<td id="S4.F4.2.15.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S4.F4.2.15.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gemini-2.5-Flash</span></td>
<td id="S4.F4.2.15.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">86.6</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.F4.2.16" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.F4.2.16.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Best SFT+<span id="S4.F4.2.16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span></td>
<td id="S4.F4.2.16.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.F4.2.16.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen2.5-7B</span></td>
<td id="S4.F4.2.16.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">86.2</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:166.9pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.23291v1/finetune_hipaa.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:208/85;" width="208" height="85"></object><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.23291v1/finetune_gdpr.svg" id="S4.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:208/85;" width="208" height="85"></object><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.23291v1/finetune_modelspec.svg" id="S4.F4.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:208/85;" width="208" height="85"></object>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S4.F4.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left</span>: We compare the best-performing baseline models, models using <span id="S4.F4.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRTs</span> as few-shot, models finetuned on <span id="S4.F4.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s, and state-of-the-art methods, including <span id="S4.F4.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GoldCoin Framework</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for HIPAA and <span id="S4.F4.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Deliberative Alignment - o1</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for ModelSpec for each policy. Using <span id="S4.F4.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s beats previous work’s performance on HIPAA by up to +1.4% while also boosting open-weight model performance to be comparable to optimized commercial models such as <span id="S4.F4.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">o1</span> with <math id="S4.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>10% difference. <span id="S4.F4.22" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right</span>: Finetuned <span id="S4.F4.23" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen-2.5-7B</span> and <span id="S4.F4.24" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">32B</span> instruct models also exhibit benefits in performance when using <span id="S4.F4.25" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s via few-shot or self feedback. Bar graphs arranged in the order from top to bottom: HIPAA, GDPR, and ModelSpec.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Interactions on Safety Optimizations and Domain Generalization</h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s May Provide Limited Gains for Doubly-Policy Optimized Models.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We observe declines in performance, averaging 4.7 in accuracy, upon using <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s with OpenAI models <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-OSS-20B</span> and <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5-Mini</span> for ModelSpec. We posit that this occurs with models we consider <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">doubly-optimized</span> using the same policy for the task of policy compliance assessment. Upon cross-checking literature, we find that OpenAI is natively optimizing models such as <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">o</span>-series, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-4</span>, and <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5</span> with RL alignment techniques such as <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Deliberative Alignment</span> with an expanded version of ModelSpec, and possibly larger, in-house safety benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Hence, these models perform better using standard prompts and yield higher results than using <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRTs</span>, and they also avoid the overthinking phenomenon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gema et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sui et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Counter to this, other commercial reasoning models like <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gemini-2.5-Flash</span> that do not specifically use ModelSpec exhibit significant positive gains using <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s as in-context demonstrations, averaging 13.0 increase in accuracy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models Finetuned on <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s Exhibit Strong Policy Generalization.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We visualize the results of our cross-policy domain generalization experiments in Figure <a href="#A7.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ Safety Optimization Interactions with PRTs ‣ Appendix G Limitations ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>. For this study, we finetune <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen2.7-7B-Instruct</span> on each policy’s <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span> train dataset and test them on each policy’s test data while exploring setups with and without <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s in-context. We observe that <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen2.7-7B-Instruct</span> finetuned on HIPAA <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span> data generalizes well to GDPR and ModelSpec test data, achieving high accuracies with 78.5 and 86.6, respectively. To compare, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-4o</span> optimized via <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Deliberative Alignment</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> obtains 88.0 on the same test set, which further supports the assistance of finetuning <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s for better generalization. Similar to using <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s as few-shots reported in Table <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Appendix A Full Tables for Inference and Training-Time Compliance Assessment ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, all models finetuned on each of the three policies separately gain doubled policy generalization performance, from 45.8 to 78.5 (+32.7) and 74.8 (+29) using GDPR data and from 36.4 to 77.6 (+41.2) and 74.3 (+37.9) using ModelSpec data, upon evaluating on the HIPAA test data when <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s are used. Conducting paired <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-test on within-policy and cross-policy values results to a non-significance<span id="footnote10" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">10</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">10</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">10</span>
                
                
                
              Within-policy mean accuracy = 0.668, cross-policy mean accuracy = 0.695; <math id="footnote10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> = –0.146, <math id="footnote10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.886.</span></span></span> in performance difference while Cohen’s <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> reveals negligible effect size (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.04) between the two groups, which suggests strong policy generalization.</p>
</div>
