<td id="A4.T8.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">torch.bfloat16</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T8.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T8.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">attn_implementation</th>
<td id="A4.T8.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">flash_attention_2</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T8.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T8.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">GPU</th>
<td id="A4.T8.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">4 x NVIDIA RTX A5000 (24GB)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>Hyperparameter settings and GPU information used for prompting LLMs.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Additional Results</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide additional supporting results on the effects of quantity of <span id="A5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s as few-shot demonstrations in Figure <a href="#A5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Effects of Model Scale. ‣ Appendix E Additional Results ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> as well as effects when models are increasing in scale or parameter size in Figure <a href="#A5.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Effects of Model Scale. ‣ Appendix E Additional Results ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Effects of Fewshot <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span> Quantity.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the few-shot <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span> quantity experiment, we do not observe a substantial difference with the default setting of three (3) we used for all our prompting experiments. For HIPAA, both finetuned <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen2.5</span> (80.4) and <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5-Mini</span> (75.7) achieved the best performance using three randomly selected <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s, surpassing any other quantity. The same applies to GDPR, but with <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5-Mini</span> (80.1) also achieving the best accuracy using three as few-shot quantities as <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen2.5</span> (74.0), without requiring finetuning. For ModelSpec, <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5-Mini</span> obtained the best performance only using two <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s (92.8) instead of three (92.1), but the difference is only 0.7 in raw points.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Effects of Model Scale.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In terms of varying model scales in Figure <a href="#A5.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Effects of Model Scale. ‣ Appendix E Additional Results ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, we observe similar patterns across three policies, where larger models tend to outperform their smaller counterparts. For HIPAA, the accumulated total gains from increasing model scale resulted in a +13.7 (mean +4.6) increase, where <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-Llama</span> obtained the most considerable boost, from 66.3 to 75.7, when scaling from 8B to 70B. Larger gains are seen with GDPR with +14.4 (mean +4.8) total boost across all models also with <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-Llama</span> being the best gainer from model scale. Lastly, using ModelSpec gets the lowest total boost of +12.2 (mean +4.2) with <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-OSS</span> being the top model. With these results, we provide a recommendation that if inference or compute budget is allowable, using larger open-weight models (typically from 7B/8B to 70B) is recommended for policy compliance assessment if higher accuracies are prioritized.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.23291v1/prt_quantity.svg" id="A5.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:466/173;" width="466" height="173"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Results from exploring quantity of <span id="A5.F8.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s as few-shot in-context demonstrations ranging from 1 to 5 comparing <span id="A5.F8.6" class="ltx_text">Qwen2.5-7B</span> models (both used via few-shot and finetuned) and an off-the-shelf commercial model <span id="A5.F8.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5-Mini</span>. </figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.23291v1/model_scale.svg" id="A5.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:466/174;" width="466" height="174"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Results from exploring performance of using <span id="A5.F9.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s as few-shot in-context demonstrations via models of increasing scale or parameter size. We explore <span id="A5.F9.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-OSS</span> (20B and 120B), <span id="A5.F9.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-Llama</span> (8B and 70B), and <span id="A5.F9.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen2.5</span> (7B and 70B) models.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Cost and Efficiency Analysis of <span id="A6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The nature of the policy compliance assessment task requires models to have considerable context length in order to fully capture both case information and the policy text without losing information. With the addition of reasoning in the form of <span id="A6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s as in-context demonstrations, the context length requires further allowance, which then translates to additional inference budget. We conduct in-depth cost and efficiency analyses of the models used for policy compliance assessments across HIPAA, GDPR, and ModelSpec to analyze the balance between accuracy and inference costs.</p>
</div>
<section id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h4>

<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Calculating the inference cost requires a uniform price point reference. For this, we used the OpenRouter API for all models in our analysis and as reported in Table <a href="#A6.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ Setup. ‣ Appendix F Cost and Efficiency Analysis of PRTs ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. We select two methods to compare inference costs, <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Few-shot</span> (no <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>) against <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Few-shot</span> (+<span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>). To determine the overall cost of running the model, we extracted the total token count for the input prompts and output texts for both methods using OpenAI’s Tiktoken tokenizer and multiplied it by USD price per 1 million input and output tokens of OpenRouter API. Note that this cost analysis is only restricted to inference costs via the API and does not include hosting the models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.T9" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A6.T9.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
