</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 9: </span>OpenRouter API (<a href="https://openrouter.ai/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://openrouter.ai/</a>) prices for all the models we used. All prices are in USD per 1M tokens.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results.</h4>

<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We visualize the Pareto frontiers of all models we evaluated across the three policies of HIPAA, GDPR, and ModelSpec in Figures <a href="#A6.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ Results. ‣ Appendix F Cost and Efficiency Analysis of PRTs ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>,  <a href="#A6.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ Results. ‣ Appendix F Cost and Efficiency Analysis of PRTs ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, and  <a href="#A6.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ Results. ‣ Appendix F Cost and Efficiency Analysis of PRTs ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, respectively.
For HIPAA, which targets compliance on health-related data protection rules, we observe that both <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen2.5-7B</span> and <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-Llama</span> are the two most cost-efficient models for policy compliance assessment, relatively comparable to the commercial frontier model <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5-Mini</span> without the higher cost per inference. In terms of tradeoff, the addition of <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRTs</span> used by the models that push the Pareto frontier further to higher accuracies than those models without using <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For GDPR, which targets compliance on general data privacy rules, where the policy text is longer, inference costs are higher than HIPAA. We observe <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-Llama</span> with no few-shot <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s is at par with <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-OSS-20B</span>. Like HIPAA, frontier reasoning models like <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5-Mini</span> offer higher accuracy while trading cost-efficiency for higher inference costs. In the context of regulatory applications, however, the particular cost requirement of more performant models, such as <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5-Mini</span> or <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-R1</span>, may be outweighed by the benefits of <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s, as interpretability and high-bar accuracy are prioritized.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For ModelSpec, which targets compliance on safe model interactions, top-performing <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>-enhanced models, such as <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gemini-2.5-Flash</span>, fail to reach the efficient frontier due to their higher inference costs. <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-OSS-20B</span> without <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s is currently the most efficient model despite lower accuracy than <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5-Mini</span> with <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s. Open-weight models like <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen2.5-7B</span> and <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-Llama</span> using <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s are well above their counterparts not using <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s in terms of accuracy without substantial loss in cost-efficiency. Similar to HIPAA and GDPR, if a modest inference budget is available, the use of higher-end frontier reasoning models such as <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5-Mini</span> or <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gemini-2.5-Flash</span> is justifiable if accuracy is prioritized. Otherwise, open-weight models like <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen2.5-7B</span> and <span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-Llama</span> are viable cost-effective options.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.23291v1/pareto_HIPAA.svg" id="A6.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:439/338;" width="439" height="338"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>Pareto frontier illustrating the trade-off between logscale inference cost against accuracy scores for models evaluating on HIPAA. Allowing for a modest inference budget, using <span id="A6.F10.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-OSS-20B</span> or <span id="A6.F10.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-Llama</span> for policy compliance assessment on HIPAA is more efficient and cheaper than <span id="A6.F10.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5-Mini</span> or <span id="A6.F10.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-R1</span> for virtually the same accuracy. The use of <span id="A6.F10.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s for HIPAA is justifiable given cost-adjusted accuracy gains with open-weight models.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A6.F11" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.23291v1/pareto_GDPR.svg" id="A6.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:439/338;" width="439" height="338"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11: </span>Pareto frontier illustrating the trade-off between logscale inference cost against accuracy scores for models evaluating on GDPR. Considering the longer policy text of GDPR, inference costs increase but certain models such as <span id="A6.F11.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-OSS-20B</span> and <span id="A6.F11.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-Llama</span> push the Pareto frontier with <span id="A6.F11.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s. The use of <span id="A6.F11.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-R1</span> and <span id="A6.F11.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5-Mini</span> is justifiable despite higher inference costs, given the context of regulatory applications where performance and interpretability are prioritized.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A6.F12" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.23291v1/pareto_ModelSpec.svg" id="A6.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:439/338;" width="439" height="338"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 12: </span>Pareto frontier illustrating the trade-off between logscale inference cost against accuracy scores for models evaluating on ModelSpec. Open-weight models such as <span id="A6.F12.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen2.5-7B</span> and <span id="A6.F12.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-Llama</span> using <span id="A6.F12.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s outperform their non-<span id="A6.F12.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span> counterparts while preserving cost-efficiency. Other commercial models like <span id="A6.F12.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5-Mini</span> are improved by <span id="A6.F12.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s in terms of performance but are not efficient due to higher inference costs.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Limitations</h2>

<section id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><span id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s As Imperfect Weak Supervision.</h4>

<div id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to how the original chain-of-thought work is not meant to provide direct gold-standard answers to tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022b</a>)</cite>, our proposed <span id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s are not meant to be treated as gold-standard references, as emphasized by the difficulty of obtaining such a resource. <span id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s function as a <span id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">scaffold</span> between case information and judgments for compliance-based tasks. While expert models generating <span id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s may produce inconsistencies and potential hallucinations, they still serve as a valuable form of weak supervision to aid learner models in connecting policy compliance nuances to judgments. We provide deeper insights into policy clause relevance scoping in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Models Finetuned on PRTs Exhibit Strong Policy Generalization. ‣ 4.2 Interactions on Safety Optimizations and Domain Generalization ‣ 4 Results ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and even analyze raw chains-of-thought of <span id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-R1</span> in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ PRTs Have High Utility Rates from Raw Chain-of-Thought. ‣ 4.3 Policy Clause Relevance and Reasoning Persistence of PRTs ‣ 4 Results ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> as forms of validation of using <span id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s for policy compliance assessment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Safety Optimization Interactions with <span id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s</h4>

<div id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Experiment Setup ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we observed an interesting phenomenon where the use of <span id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s with doubly-policy optimized models for safety using ModelSpec with OpenAI models like <span id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5-Mini</span> results in a slight deterioration in performance. We believe this is an interesting orthogonal research direction that can be explored in future work, assuming the availability of comprehensive publicly available safety-related policies and compliance assessment datasets beyond ModelSpec. Nonetheless, we still observed the benefits of <span id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRTs</span> for safety compliance on non-OpenAI models, such as <span id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gemini-2.5-Flash</span>, <span id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen2.5-7B</span>, and <span id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A7.F13" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.23291v1/generalization_confusion_matrices.svg" id="A7.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:466/130;" width="466" height="130"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13: </span>Policy generalization results using separately-finetuned <span id="A7.F13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen2.5-7B</span> models using <span id="A7.F13.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span> training data for HIPAA, GDPR, and ModelSpec. The labels on the y-axis denote the models trained from the source policy, and the labels on the x-axis denote the target policy where the finetuned model is evaluated. As with all the experiments, the target policy’s entire policy text is provided during inference.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A8" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix H </span>Prompts</h2>

<div id="A8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide two types of prompts used in the main experiments of our paper. Inference prompts are used across inference-based experiments where model performances are recorded as reported in Figures <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Learner Models and Assessment Methods. ‣ 3 Experiment Setup ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ PRTs Sets New SOTA for Legal Policy Compliance Assessment. ‣ 4.1 Inference-Time and Training Time Policy Compliance Assessment ‣ 4 Results ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, and  <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ PRTs Sets New SOTA for Legal Policy Compliance Assessment. ‣ 4.1 Inference-Time and Training Time Policy Compliance Assessment ‣ 4 Results ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and Tables <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Appendix A Full Tables for Inference and Training-Time Compliance Assessment ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and  <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix A Full Tables for Inference and Training-Time Compliance Assessment ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Utility prompts, on the other hand, are used for supporting experiments that require additional analysis, including prompts for extracting policy clause relevance in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Models Finetuned on PRTs Exhibit Strong Policy Generalization. ‣ 4.2 Interactions on Safety Optimizations and Domain Generalization ‣ 4 Results ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <span id="A8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span> utilization in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ PRTs Have High Utility Rates from Raw Chain-of-Thought. ‣ 4.3 Policy Clause Relevance and Reasoning Persistence of PRTs ‣ 4 Results ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, and using expert models to generate <span id="A8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PRT</span>s as seen in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Policies and Test Datasets. ‣ 3 Experiment Setup ‣ Scaling Policy Compliance Assessment in Language Models with Policy Reasoning Traces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A8.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">H.1 </span>Inference Prompts</h3>

